            0.151,
            0.326,
            0.847,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since ABCD is a rhombus, all its sides have equal length, and the diagonals are perpendicular bisectors of each other. Therefore, ∆ABD and ∆CBD are isosceles triangles. Each of them can be transformed into a rectangle of equal area, and the two rectangles can then be joined to form a single rectangle. This rectangle, say WXYZ, has the same area as the rhombus ABCD."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.44,
            0.629,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What are the sidelengths of the rectangle WXYZ?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.465,
            0.513,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From the dissection, we can see that"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.322,
            0.491,
            0.677,
            0.51
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "XW = length of the diagonal AC, and"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.324,
            0.51,
            0.77,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "WZ = half the length of the other diagonal BD."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.535,
            0.299,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, we have"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.564,
            0.75,
            0.672
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\underset{\\textcircled{C}}{\\operatorname{Area}} \\text { of} \\underset{\\text {hom}}{\\operatorname{hom}} \\underset{\\text {is }}{\\operatorname{ABCD}} & \\overset{\\rightharpoonup }{=} \\underset{\\text {A}}{\\operatorname{free}} \\text { of } \\underset{\\text {rectangle }}{\\operatorname{WXYZ}} \\\\& = \\underset{\\text {XW}}{\\operatorname{XW}} \\times \\underset{\\text {WZ}}{\\operatorname{WZ}} \\\\& = \\underset{\\text {AC}}{\\operatorname{AC}} \\times \\frac{\\mathrm{BD}}{2} \\\\& = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\underset{\\text {AC}}{\\operatorname{AC}} \\times \\mathrm{BD}\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.666,
            0.261,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.28,
            0.691,
            0.715,
            0.719
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "\\scriptstyle { \\overline { { A r e a \\; o f { \\overline { { a } } } \\; r h o m b u s } } } \\; = \\; { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } \\; \\times \\; p r o d u c t \\; o f d i a g o n a l s ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.745,
            0.847,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Area of rhombus ABCD can also be determined by finding the areas of ∆ADB and ∆CDB. What formula does this give us?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.791,
            0.667,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since the diagonals are perpendicular to each other, we have"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.832,
            0.566,
            0.859
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_13.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{Area}(\\Delta \\mathrm{ADB})=\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\mathrm{AO} \\times \\mathrm{BD}, \\text { and }"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.865,
            0.566,
            0.892
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{Area}(\\Delta \\mathrm{CDB})=\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\mathrm{CO} \\times \\mathrm{BD}, \\mathrm{and}"
        },
